{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793810862,"nonce":"UIUXb1Y+bBC5V+JMVeSLFZwDo40=","message":"Committed e7dd78cafd39396f7e25dc52190b49781abb7d4c.\n\nOSR now preserves non-null live-in proofs from the source body and available constructor bodies, without optimizing or mutating the source to enter the interpreter. The shared simplifier consumes existing nonnull parameter attributes. The return analysis also handles retained bodies marked optimized without a saved return fact. No noalias attributes or snapshot-format fields were added. Partial escape now accepts a single allocation combined with an opaque incoming object, including webs that have no consuming escape; existing alias, mutation, identity and layout checks still apply.\n\nNUC paired medians (11 samples per arm, frozen binaries, exclusive lock, cooldown and per-sample thermal/load gates, no Cargo/rustc during sampling):\n- op_overload, LLVM off: wall 188.868 -\u003e 32.720 ms (5.77x); internal timer 172.465 -\u003e 19.056 ms.\n- op_overload, LLVM enabled: wall 193.051 -\u003e 38.950 ms (4.96x); internal timer 175.640 -\u003e 23.713 ms.\n- method_call, LLVM off: wall 29.100 -\u003e 29.512 ms; extended run 53.708 -\u003e 53.740 ms.\n- method_call, LLVM enabled: wall 31.436 -\u003e 31.537 ms; extended run 57.106 -\u003e 58.210 ms.\n- mandelbrot: wall 48.521 -\u003e 48.614 ms; lambda_call 10.784 -\u003e 10.423 ms.\nThese compare against interpreter-first commit 4dac9a22436df3dee5dac27a47a8c28bd7ada8c2. They do not claim a win over LuaJIT or the earlier blocking-entry path. LLVM-enabled names the configuration.\n\nSeparate untimed NUC traces confirm actual interpreter-to-native OSR in both versions with LLVM off and ordinary snapshot eligibility. The optimized op_overload region shrinks from 571 to 255 dump lines: six malloc sites (including the two hot result allocations) and two generic zl_arith calls become zero of each. All 18 Lua benchmark rows and 22 supported Python kernels match checksums; pidigits remains unsupported. Three Lua samples and one Python sample per arm in the wider sweep are exploratory. Follow-up five-pair Python wall medians: scimark_lu 222.308 -\u003e 224.969 ms, raytrace-simple 308.098 -\u003e 310.778 ms, spectral-norm 62.159 -\u003e 62.926 ms. No thermal-throttle deltas were observed.\n\nValidation: 36 focused compiler tests (including non-null/nullable-backedge proofs, single-allocation opaque-input loops executed with interpreter/Cranelift/LLVM, existing alias/mutation/identity tests and OSR destination-return tests); Clippy with warnings denied; 57 Lua tests including complete official and resume-point tests; 50 Python conformance tests; 1,413 local TS cases across interpreter/baseline/LLVM profiles. Complete pinned official TS corpus: 5,948/5,948 files, 6,825 rows, 1,151 PASS, 12 existing FAIL, 2,234 UNSUPPORTED and 3,428 SKIP; zero per-case baseline regressions, no baseline changes.\n\nEvidence: /private/tmp/zyntax-osr-facts/nuc-evidence.tar.gz, SHA-256 1f3a1ad8d52a0e2afbb636cade94cdb1688e54c38bfd11714ec8dbbfad218a8c. Raw medians, IR, tier traces, thermal records, scripts and binary hashes are archived there; local validation logs, complete TS JSON reports and final source hashes are alongside it. NUC production-source hashes match the locally validated files. Original NUC source checkout and unrelated local work were preserved. No push performed.\n\nClose this bounded optimization. Keep 60a9e71ab07f59f416ea7336ab573e807e1f068d05cf91ca64cdeb163a4b9b19 open: method_call still needs safe allocation/global disjointness provenance across OSR, and non-nullness alone cannot supply it.","files":null}]}